**Proof.**

We must show that $f = g$ (as functions $X \to Y$) if and only if
$$
(f(x), y)_Y = (g(x), y)_Y \quad \text{for all } x \in X,\; y \in Y.
$$

---

**($\Longrightarrow$) Forward direction.**

Assume $f = g$. Then for every $x \in X$ we have $f(x) = g(x)$ as elements of $Y$. Hence for every $y \in Y$,
$$
(f(x), y)_Y = (g(x), y)_Y
$$
by the well-definedness of the inner product: equal inputs to $(\cdot, \cdot)_Y$ give equal outputs. This holds for all $x \in X$ and all $y \in Y$, which is exactly the desired equality.

---

**($\Longleftarrow$) Reverse direction.**

Assume that for all $x \in X$ and all $y \in Y$,
$$
(f(x), y)_Y = (g(x), y)_Y.
$$

Fix an arbitrary $x \in X$ and set $h := f(x) - g(x) \in Y$. Subtracting, the hypothesis gives
$$
(h, y)_Y = 0 \quad \text{for all } y \in Y.
$$

In particular, taking $y = h$ (which is allowed since $h \in Y$),
$$
(h, h)_Y = 0.
$$

By positive definiteness of the inner product on the Hilbert space $Y$, $(h, h)_Y = 0$ implies $h = 0$, i.e.,
$$
f(x) - g(x) = 0 \quad \Longrightarrow \quad f(x) = g(x).
$$

Since $x \in X$ was arbitrary, $f(x) = g(x)$ for all $x \in X$, which is precisely $f = g$.

---

Combining both directions, $f = g$ if and only if $(f(x), y)_Y = (g(x), y)_Y$ for all $x \in X$ and $y \in Y$. $\blacksquare$

